  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e7e6a7a7e46c14191819aae88fd283a2, type: 3}
  m_Name: New Dialog Data 1
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  Dialog: "\"You know, I\u2019m glad we chose this place. It\u2019s quiet. My morning
    was the exact opposite\u2014absolute tectonic chaos.\n\nIt started when I found
    a literal rift in the space-time continuum behind my toaster. I knew I shouldn't
    have bought the budget brand. I reached back there to find a dropped crust, and
    instead, I accidentally pulled out a translucent, glowing version of my cat,
    Barnaby.\n\nThe 'Real' Barnaby was not thrilled. He spent the next hour trying
    to hiss at his own fourth-dimensional shadow while I tried to explain to my sister
    on the phone that I couldn't come to her gender reveal party because my kitchen
    was currently a gateway to the Year 3000.\n\nMy family, bless them, didn't even
    blink. My sister just said, 'Well, as long as the rift is blue or pink, just
    bring a dip.'\n\nI gave up on the phone call because that\u2019s when the Neon
    Shogunate arrived. They didn't come from the door; they folded themselves out
    of the shadows of my kitchen cabinets. It was the start of the 'Great Indigo
    Uprising'\u2014a ninja revolution fueled by sentient shadows.\n\nThey weren't
    looking for money; they wanted my secret recipe for heirloom tomato sauce. Apparently,
    in the shadow dimension, acidity is the only thing that can power their mecha-suits.\n\nI
    had to defend the stovetop. I grabbed two silver ladles and channeled every ounce
    of the fencing lessons my dad forced me to take in middle school. It was a blur
    of silk, smoke, and garlic powder. I was parrying katanas with cookware, dodging
    throwing stars that turned into butterflies upon impact.\n\nThe turning point
    was when Barnaby\u2014the ghost one\u2014realized he could eat their smoke bombs.
    He started glowing like a disco ball and floating around the room, which confused
    the ninjas long enough for me to trap their leader in a Tupperware container.\n\nI
    negotiated a peace treaty right there on the linoleum. They promised to leave
    our dimension alone if I gave them a jar of Prego and a signed photo of Gordon
    Ramsay.\n\nBy 3:00 PM, the rift closed, the shadow ninjas evaporated into a light
    lavender mist, and I finally got to finish my laundry. Honestly, the hardest
    part of the whole day was finding a pair of heels that didn't have scorch marks
    on them for tonight.\n\nBut hey, the world is safe, the kitchen is clean, and
    the wine is chilled. I\u2019d call that a win.\""
  RightAnswerReply: "\"Oh, wow\u2014you actually were listening! I\u2019m impressed.
    Most guys usually tune out the second I mention 'space-time continuum' or my
    cat\u2019s dietary restrictions. It\u2019s a relief, honestly, because if the
    Shogunate ever returns, I need a partner who knows the terms of the ceasefire.
    That photo was actually a prized possession of mine; I won it in a cooking competition
    back in college, but sacrificing it for the survival of the human race felt like
    the right move. Gordon\u2019s forehead wrinkles apparently have a very calming,
    almost religious effect on shadow-ninjas. It\u2019s nice to know I\u2019m sitting
    across from someone who values the details. It makes me feel like I don't have
    to filter the weirdness, you know? Plus, it means you\u2019re officially qualified
    to help me if the toaster starts acting up again on our second date. How about
    we split the lava cake to celebrate your excellent memory?\""
  WrongAnswerReply: "\"Wait, really? The artichoke dip? Honestly, I\u2019m a little
    insulted on behalf of the ninjas. Do you really think a high-ranking shadow assassin
    from the fourth dimension would be satisfied with a mayonnaise-based appetizer?
    That dip is my sister\u2019s pride and joy, but it doesn't have nearly enough
    'culinary authority' to stop a revolution. It sounds like you might have drifted
    off into a daydream while I was describing the literal folding of reality in
    my kitchen! I get it, the wine is strong and I talk fast, but we\u2019re talking
    about the safety of the planet here. Now I\u2019m worried that if a rift opened
    up right under this table, you\u2019d be too busy thinking about spinach and
    cheese to grab my hand and jump through. I guess I\u2019ll have to tell the story
    again, but this time I\u2019m using the salt and pepper shakers to act out the
    battle scenes so you stay focused. Pay attention\u2014this part involves the
    ladles!\""
  Question: "So, just to make sure you aren't thinking I\u2019m totally crazy...
    according to my 'treaty' with the Neon Shogunate, what was the specific item
    I had to give them\u2014along with the tomato sauce\u2014to save our dimension?"
  Answers:
  - A vintage map of the Tokyo subway system.
  - A signed photo of Gordon Ramsay.
  - "My sister\u2019s secret recipe for artichoke dip."
  - A glowing whisker from the ghost of Barnaby.
  RightAnswer: 1
